implementationPlan.description:
THE REFUSAL ARMS — DoD item 2's malformed and unreadable cases. Every one asserts the exact stable code AND which of the two layers answered, because the adapter's own contract header states two layers can refuse and that restamping an upstream code is forbidden.
AT `DAEMON_IMPORT_SHADOW`:
- a `manifestDigest` that is not 64 lowercase hex -> `IMPORT_SHADOW_INPUT_INVALID`;
- a manifest digest with no committed aggregate -> `IMPORT_SHADOW_ABSENT`;
- a store port whose `readEvents` throws -> `IMPORT_SHADOW_STORE_UNREADABLE`, and separately one whose `readEvents` answers a non-list, which must also refuse rather than crash on `.length` — the adapter comments that a crash is not a refusal, so assert the refusal, not a thrown error;
- a horizon that moves between the adapter's two reads -> `IMPORT_SHADOW_HORIZON_DRIFT`, with NO partial comparison returned;
- legacy facts whose shape `projectLegacyImport` throws on -> `IMPORT_SHADOW_LEGACY_UNREADABLE`. This one is worth its own note: the legacy side is caller data, and `compareImportShadow` wraps the projection in a try/catch for exactly this reason. Assert the refusal AND that no comparison object came back.
UPSTREAM-PRESERVED, asserted with the IMPORTER's own code and layer, never a daemon code: a committed row whose payload bytes were tampered to decode-fail, and one whose facts schema version is unsupported.
A test asserting only `ok === false` would stay green if the daemon layer started answering first and swallowed the importer's finer code — that is precisely the regression these layer assertions exist to catch.
After EVERY refusal arm, assert the store's raw event and decision counts and the database's bytes and mtime are unchanged (DoD item 4). A handler that refused after writing is invisible to a return-value assertion.
